            <ArticleTitle>Do different vascular risk factors affect all arteries equally?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>397-401</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0003319708318383</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Established vascular risk factors (ie, smoking, hypertension, diabetes mellitus, and dyslipidemia) play an important role in the development of vascular disease. Emerging evidence suggests that some of these risk factors may have a more intense effect on specific arterial beds, a finding that holds implications for a prognostic role for certain types of vascular disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
